## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end understanding of a retrieval-augmented QA flow using embeddings, chunking, and a local vector store. It also probes data freshness considerations and how to validate recall in a practical setup.

## Key Concepts
- Chunking with overlap to preserve context
- Embeddings and cosine similarity for retrieval
- Local vector stores (FAISS-like) for fast lookups
- Data freshness via timestamps and TTL with refresh strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: basic retrieve flow
function retrieve(query){
  const qEmb = embed(query);
  const top = vecStore.topK(qEmb, 2);
  return top.map(chunk => chunk.text).join(" ");
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you tune chunk size and overlap for different docs?
- How would you measure recall and latency in this setup?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of retrieval basics: chunking, embeddings, and vector stores in a compact, beginner‑friendly setup.

## Key Concepts
- Chunking strategy, embedding model choice, cosine similarity
- In-memory vector store for low latency
- Simple re-ranking using exact-term hits
- Latency and memory considerations in small-scale apps

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal pseudo-code: chunk, embed, store, search
async function chunk(text, size=400){ /* split into chunks */ }
async function embed(text){ /* call embedding API */ }
async function search(query, chunks){
  const q = await embed(query);
  const sims = chunks.map(c => ({ chunk: c, score: cosine(q, c.vec) }));
  sims.sort((a,b) => b.score - a.score);
  // simple re-ranking by term matches
  return sims.slice(0,3).sort((a,b)=>hitScore(b.chunk, query) - hitScore(a.chunk, query));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale the vector store for larger corpora?
- What strategies would you use to handle multi-language docs or frequent updates?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to implement a minimal retrieval loop that works with embeddings, chunking, and a vector-like store, while considering multilingual routing and update freshness.

## Key Concepts

- In-memory vector store
- Text chunking with overlap
- Embedding generation
- Cosine similarity search
- Multilingual handling
- Update freshness and caching

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo JS snippet: minimal in-memory index
const model = loadModel('all-MiniLM-L6-v2');
function chunk(text, max=300, overlap=50){ /* split and overlap */ }
let index = [];
function indexDocs(docs){
  docs.forEach(doc=>{
    chunk(doc.text).forEach(ch=>{
      index.push({id: doc.id+':' + hash(ch), text: ch, emb: model.encode(ch)});
    });
  });
}
function query(q, topK=5){
  const qEmb = model.encode(q);
  return index
    .map(n => ({...n, score: cosine(n.emb, qEmb)}))
    .sort((a,b)=> b.score-a.score)
    .slice(0, topK);
}
```

